1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: It explained the photoelectric effect, and later won him a Nobel Prize.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: That raises a question. What happens to the stripes when light is so dim that it arrives a little at a time?

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: In 1909, a young Cambridge physicist, Geoffrey Ingram Taylor, tried it.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: He dimmed a gas light with dark glass, until it was like a candle more than a mile away.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: A candle more than a mile off? That's faint.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: It was so faint that his photographic plate, behind a sewing needle, took three months to expose.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: And the fringes were still there.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: As Paul Dirac later put it, each photon then interferes only with itself.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: Interferes with itself. That's a strange sentence.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: It is. And one caution is that Taylor's paper never mentions photons, and his dim lamp wasn't a true single-photon source.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: So it was very faint light, not strictly one photon at a time.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: The real test came in nineteen eighty-six, when a French team including Alain Aspect used a genuine single-photon source and an interferometer.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: And the interference was still there, one photon at a time.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: Here, each photon is detected at one single point.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: A few hundred look like noise.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: But by ten thousand, the stripes are unmistakable.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: And with brighter light?

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: The dots just pile up faster, giving the same pattern, sooner.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: Electrons came the other way round. From eighteen ninety-seven, experiments showed them behaving as particles.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: Then, in 1924, Louis de Broy proposed that all matter has a wave side too.
